  "background_and_question": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "Festo Corporation (Petitioner)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "764460c4beaa03fd300d4b55df997ea234370cd16fe951fd876500480c030e98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-764460c4beaa-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Festo Corporation (Petitioner), petitioner argues that the Federal Circuit's rules should be reversed because they contradict the Supreme Court\u2019s decision in Warner\u2011Jenkinson and undermine the patent system."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "764460c4beaa03fd300d4b55df997ea234370cd16fe951fd876500480c030e98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-764460c4beaa-0000-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 1,
              "start": 1
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to United States (Amicus Curiae), the United States, as amicus curiae, supports vacatur and remand of the case."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "United States",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "764460c4beaa03fd300d4b55df997ea234370cd16fe951fd876500480c030e98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-764460c4beaa-0001-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 21,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to United States, amicus counsel Lawrence G. Wallace explains that amendments made to avoid prior art are typically narrowing, whereas other amendments are not, and contends that the Federal Circuit\u2019s discussion of \u201cnarrowing amendments\u201d mischaracterizes the petitioner\u2019s position."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Neustadt",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "764460c4beaa03fd300d4b55df997ea234370cd16fe951fd876500480c030e98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-764460c4beaa-0001-0004"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 34,
              "start": 34
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Respondent, through Mr. Neustadt, argues that the Federal Circuit\u2019s \u201cflexible bar\u201d rule improperly permits a doctrine\u2011of\u2011equivalents claim by allowing a party to equate \u201cglue\u201d with \u201cadhesives,\u201d and that such practice should not be allowed."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Neustadt",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "764460c4beaa03fd300d4b55df997ea234370cd16fe951fd876500480c030e98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-764460c4beaa-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 36,
              "start": 36
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Mr. Neustadt estimates that roughly fifty percent or more of patent applications involve the issues under discussion."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "764460c4beaa03fd300d4b55df997ea234370cd16fe951fd876500480c030e98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-764460c4beaa-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Justice asks whether there is a relevant difference between claim elements and claim limitations."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "764460c4beaa03fd300d4b55df997ea234370cd16fe951fd876500480c030e98",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-764460c4beaa-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 36,
              "start": 36
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice (unspecified), justice asks whether transferring the dispute to the Patent Office\u2019s appeals process will result in a long, protracted timeline."
      }
    ],
